WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.979 --> 00:00:04.431
- Hello Believe Nation, it's Evan.

00:00:04.431 --> 00:00:05.681
My one word is believe,

00:00:05.681 --> 00:00:07.839
and I believe in people

00:00:07.839 --> 00:00:09.932
more than they believe in themselves,

00:00:09.932 --> 00:00:11.204
and my sincere hope is that

00:00:11.204 --> 00:00:14.771
if you see in yourself what I see in you,

00:00:14.771 --> 00:00:17.441
you'll be able to go off
and change the planet.

00:00:17.441 --> 00:00:18.965
Now, I started the Believe Love series,

00:00:18.965 --> 00:00:21.232
because being in a
relationship can be hard

00:00:21.232 --> 00:00:22.674
and being an entrepreneur can be hard.

00:00:22.674 --> 00:00:24.610
And you combine both of
those two things together,

00:00:24.610 --> 00:00:26.975
life gets a lot more difficult

00:00:26.975 --> 00:00:28.824
and I thought it would be a great gift

00:00:28.824 --> 00:00:30.653
for myself and Nina, for our relationship,

00:00:30.653 --> 00:00:34.411
as well as for you guys to
every week explore new topics,

00:00:34.411 --> 00:00:36.942
new ways to try to make
your relationships better.

00:00:36.942 --> 00:00:39.999
So, today we're going to
learn from Jordan B. Peterson

00:00:39.999 --> 00:00:42.416
on how to train your partner.

00:00:43.421 --> 00:00:45.674
- You're having an
argument with your partner.

00:00:45.674 --> 00:00:48.706
And you say, "You're a stupid person

00:00:48.706 --> 00:00:50.677
"and you've always been a stupid person

00:00:50.677 --> 00:00:52.433
"and, as far as I can tell,

00:00:52.433 --> 00:00:54.273
"as far into the future as I can see,

00:00:54.273 --> 00:00:57.094
"you're going to remain a stupid person."

00:00:57.094 --> 00:00:58.304
So, what are they supposed to do?

00:00:58.304 --> 00:01:01.227
What are they going to
do when you say that?

00:01:01.227 --> 00:01:04.411
They're going to cry, like if you mean it.

00:01:04.411 --> 00:01:07.470
They're going to get
angry, if you mean it.

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:09.612
And they're not going
to like you very much.

00:01:09.612 --> 00:01:10.606
And why is that?

00:01:10.606 --> 00:01:14.032
Well, it's like, it's assault, basically.

00:01:14.032 --> 00:01:16.541
The only way really, the
only thing that you can do

00:01:16.541 --> 00:01:20.217
in a situation like that
is walk away, ignore it,

00:01:20.217 --> 00:01:23.835
respond in kind, or it
degenerates into violence.

00:01:23.835 --> 00:01:24.839
That's it.

00:01:24.839 --> 00:01:26.171
Because there's no discussion.

00:01:26.171 --> 00:01:27.846
You haven't left the
person anywhere to go.

00:01:27.846 --> 00:01:30.365
You've gone right to the
top of their hierarchy

00:01:30.365 --> 00:01:32.600
and said everything about you is wrong,

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:34.719
and worse than that, all the mechanisms

00:01:34.719 --> 00:01:37.578
that we could use to
correct it won't work.

00:01:37.578 --> 00:01:39.740
So, those are fighting words.

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:41.912
So, don't do that, unless
you want to have a fight.

00:01:41.912 --> 00:01:44.140
So then you might say, well
what would you do instead?

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:46.416
And the answer is deliver the least amount

00:01:46.416 --> 00:01:49.048
of information you possibly can.

00:01:49.048 --> 00:01:51.597
And so, let's say, you come home

00:01:51.597 --> 00:01:53.445
and your person is watching TV

00:01:53.445 --> 00:01:55.944
and you were kind of hoping
they'd greet you at the door.

00:01:55.944 --> 00:01:57.834
You shouldn't break down into tears

00:01:57.834 --> 00:01:59.419
and say, you're a stupid person.

00:01:59.419 --> 00:02:00.739
You've always been a stupid person.

00:02:00.739 --> 00:02:02.611
You're going to be a stupid
person in the future.

00:02:02.611 --> 00:02:05.861
You should say, I have this peculiarity

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:08.901
and that is that, when I come home,

00:02:08.901 --> 00:02:12.791
I don't have enough
confidence to just be happy.

00:02:12.791 --> 00:02:14.541
I would like you to come and say,

00:02:14.541 --> 00:02:16.450
just shut the TV off for two minutes,

00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:18.909
come to the front door, and say hello

00:02:18.909 --> 00:02:20.766
and then you can go
back and watch your TV.

00:02:20.766 --> 00:02:22.729
Would it be okay, if you did that?

00:02:22.729 --> 00:02:27.177
And they'll think, well, you'll
have to pay for it somehow.

00:02:27.177 --> 00:02:29.940
But then they'll probably do it.

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:31.290
And so, but the thing is you've got

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:34.390
to specify the routine
that you want transformed

00:02:34.390 --> 00:02:36.959
at the highest possible level
of resolution and you want

00:02:36.959 --> 00:02:41.134
to recommend the minimal necessary change

00:02:41.134 --> 00:02:43.228
that will satisfy you.

00:02:43.228 --> 00:02:46.628
So you can't say, if you loved me,

00:02:46.628 --> 00:02:48.852
you'd know how to greet me at the door.

00:02:48.852 --> 00:02:51.928
Ha, not helpful, because they're stupid.

00:02:51.928 --> 00:02:52.761
(audience laughs)

00:02:52.761 --> 00:02:54.401
Right, and so are you!

00:02:54.401 --> 00:02:55.732
So you have to spell it out.

00:02:55.732 --> 00:02:56.790
It's like, what do you want?

00:02:56.790 --> 00:02:59.582
Exactly, what would make you satisfied?

00:02:59.582 --> 00:03:02.032
And then you have to
have your person, like,

00:03:02.032 --> 00:03:04.714
grudgingly practice that a few times

00:03:04.714 --> 00:03:06.551
and you have to let them do it very badly

00:03:06.551 --> 00:03:09.955
and also in a bad temper and
then you have to reward them

00:03:09.955 --> 00:03:12.942
for it and then, maybe three months later,

00:03:12.942 --> 00:03:14.791
they'll do it properly.

00:03:14.791 --> 00:03:15.955
So, you need to know that,

00:03:15.955 --> 00:03:17.392
because that's what people are like.

00:03:17.392 --> 00:03:19.148
It's very hard for them
to learn new things

00:03:19.148 --> 00:03:20.651
and they're very resistant to it,

00:03:20.651 --> 00:03:23.536
but they're very responsive to reward.

00:03:23.536 --> 00:03:24.633
So, another thing,

00:03:24.633 --> 00:03:26.970
this is partly what B.F.
Skinner figured out,

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:29.916
so when he was training
rats and he wanted a rat

00:03:29.916 --> 00:03:30.749
to do something,

00:03:30.749 --> 00:03:32.635
one of the things he would do is he put,

00:03:32.635 --> 00:03:37.250
maybe he was going to train a
rat to climb a little ladder.

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:39.192
I mean, he could get rats to climb ladders

00:03:39.192 --> 00:03:41.070
and then climb across, like,
the little monkey bars,

00:03:41.070 --> 00:03:43.903
and then spin around three
times and then whack a ball

00:03:43.903 --> 00:03:44.787
and then eat something.

00:03:44.787 --> 00:03:48.251
Like, he got incredibly
complex behavior out of rats.

00:03:48.251 --> 00:03:50.985
And the way he did that was patience.

00:03:50.985 --> 00:03:52.773
So he put the ladder in the cage

00:03:52.773 --> 00:03:55.342
and the rat would just run
around, doing rat things.

00:03:55.342 --> 00:03:59.141
Then, it would put its
hand on the first rung

00:03:59.141 --> 00:04:01.692
and Skinner would give it a pellet.

00:04:01.692 --> 00:04:03.976
And so, he did it after.

00:04:03.976 --> 00:04:07.065
Even once, the rat's
going to be like stand

00:04:07.065 --> 00:04:09.718
in the immediate vicinity of the ladder

00:04:09.718 --> 00:04:12.275
and then the frequency with
which it's going to go like this

00:04:12.275 --> 00:04:13.108
has just increased.

00:04:13.108 --> 00:04:14.228
So then he does it again.

00:04:14.228 --> 00:04:15.377
Bang, pellet!

00:04:15.377 --> 00:04:18.331
Well, soon, the rat is just
going like this, right?

00:04:18.331 --> 00:04:21.420
So then, you wait until the
rat tries the other hand.

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:22.793
So you give it a pellet then.

00:04:22.793 --> 00:04:24.520
Well, then it's going like this.

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:25.900
And then, because it's going to get bored,

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:27.055
it'll go like this.

00:04:27.055 --> 00:04:27.888
It'll hit the next stair.

00:04:27.888 --> 00:04:28.931
Bang, you give it a pellet.

00:04:28.931 --> 00:04:29.906
Soon the rat's climbing

00:04:29.906 --> 00:04:32.342
and doing all the little
things you want it to do.

00:04:32.342 --> 00:04:35.108
Now, the problem with that
is you have to be patient.

00:04:35.108 --> 00:04:37.735
You have to wait 'til the
rat does what you want.

00:04:37.735 --> 00:04:40.268
Okay, that's more relationship advice.

00:04:40.268 --> 00:04:41.101
(audience laughs)

00:04:41.101 --> 00:04:44.262
Wait 'til the rat does what
you want and then reward it.

00:04:44.262 --> 00:04:46.923
And it's unbelievably useful.

00:04:46.923 --> 00:04:48.755
It's also extraordinarily positive.

00:04:48.755 --> 00:04:51.838
I mean, I'm being, you know, comical,

00:04:52.944 --> 00:04:55.462
in so far as I can manage
that, about this situation.

00:04:55.462 --> 00:04:59.576
But people love reward
and they love attention.

00:04:59.576 --> 00:05:01.833
People love attention
more than anything else.

00:05:01.833 --> 00:05:04.991
And so, if you watch through the day

00:05:04.991 --> 00:05:08.088
and when your partner does
something that's good,

00:05:08.088 --> 00:05:10.819
say, man that was good,
or something like that.

00:05:10.819 --> 00:05:12.090
You can be inventive.

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:13.704
And then they'll do it more.

00:05:13.704 --> 00:05:17.028
And if you do that a whole
bunch, like for a year,

00:05:17.028 --> 00:05:18.580
they'll be doing things
that are good for you,

00:05:18.580 --> 00:05:20.165
just all the time.

00:05:20.165 --> 00:05:23.679
But you have to be patient,
which is very annoying,

00:05:23.679 --> 00:05:27.012
and you have to suppress
your response to only respond

00:05:27.012 --> 00:05:28.820
to negative things.

00:05:28.820 --> 00:05:30.251
You know, because what we know

00:05:30.251 --> 00:05:34.168
about the expectancy
models is that a deviation

00:05:35.168 --> 00:05:38.878
from expectation produces a
burst of negative emotions.

00:05:38.878 --> 00:05:41.824
You know, so you come home
and the whole house is clean,

00:05:41.824 --> 00:05:46.192
but there's, like, I don't
know, the dog has shed

00:05:46.192 --> 00:05:48.447
on the rug or something and
the person overlooked that.

00:05:48.447 --> 00:05:49.788
It's not like you're going
to see the clean house,

00:05:49.788 --> 00:05:52.664
you're going to see the
rug with the dog fur on it.

00:05:52.664 --> 00:05:54.814
You're going to say, why
didn't you clean up the rug

00:05:54.814 --> 00:05:55.720
with the dog fur?

00:05:55.720 --> 00:05:57.456
And they're going to
say, good luck getting me

00:05:57.456 --> 00:05:59.235
to clean up the house again.

00:05:59.235 --> 00:06:03.519
And, you know, because the thing
is the exception stands out

00:06:03.519 --> 00:06:05.501
and what's done doesn't,

00:06:05.501 --> 00:06:07.856
and the reason for that is you
can just ignore what's done,

00:06:07.856 --> 00:06:08.720
because it's done.

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:10.012
It doesn't get in your way.

00:06:10.012 --> 00:06:11.768
So, it gets invisible really quickly.

00:06:11.768 --> 00:06:15.245
So, you really got to watch that tendency.

00:06:15.245 --> 00:06:17.041
One of the things Nietzsche said was that,

00:06:17.041 --> 00:06:19.195
if you really want to punish someone,

00:06:19.195 --> 00:06:21.827
you don't punish them when
they do something wrong,

00:06:21.827 --> 00:06:23.003
because they expect that.

00:06:23.003 --> 00:06:24.507
That's not a punishment!

00:06:24.507 --> 00:06:25.340
They expect that!

00:06:25.340 --> 00:06:27.639
They might even be relieved by it.

00:06:27.639 --> 00:06:30.449
You want to punish them when
they do something right,

00:06:30.449 --> 00:06:32.129
because then you'll really hurt them.

00:06:32.129 --> 00:06:33.841
And so, that's something to think about,

00:06:33.841 --> 00:06:35.989
if you're in a relationship, man.

00:06:35.989 --> 00:06:39.635
If someone's done something
right, do not punish them.

00:06:39.635 --> 00:06:41.768
You do that two or three times, that's it.

00:06:41.768 --> 00:06:44.450
You're not going to get
them to do that anymore.

00:06:44.450 --> 00:06:45.950
So, judiciousness.

00:06:46.929 --> 00:06:48.667
Watch what they're doing.

00:06:48.667 --> 00:06:51.430
If something happens
that's good, notice it.

00:06:51.430 --> 00:06:53.247
And, you know, if they've
done a bunch of things,

00:06:53.247 --> 00:06:55.866
don't concentrate on the
things they did wrong.

00:06:55.866 --> 00:06:57.392
That's not smart.

00:06:57.392 --> 00:06:59.029
It's really hard on them too.

00:06:59.029 --> 00:07:02.926
In some sense, this sounds
manipulative and selfish,

00:07:02.926 --> 00:07:06.080
because I'm teaching you
how to train your partner.

00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:07.312
(audience laughs)

00:07:07.312 --> 00:07:10.562
But you should also teach
them how to train you.

00:07:10.562 --> 00:07:11.628
Because it would be really nice,

00:07:11.628 --> 00:07:13.570
if you could come home
and the person would say,

00:07:13.570 --> 00:07:14.405
What did you do today?

00:07:14.405 --> 00:07:17.528
And you say, here's a bunch
of things I did and they say,

00:07:17.528 --> 00:07:19.377
well, this looks really
good and that was great,

00:07:19.377 --> 00:07:20.557
and why don't you do some more of that?

00:07:20.557 --> 00:07:21.919
And you're like, oh boy!

00:07:21.919 --> 00:07:23.282
(audience laughs)
It was a great day!

00:07:23.282 --> 00:07:28.159
And so, you know, you can train
them to train you properly.

00:07:28.159 --> 00:07:29.874
And that's a really helpful thing,

00:07:29.874 --> 00:07:31.520
especially if you do it over a few years.

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:34.373
You know, that's how you
have a good relationship,

00:07:34.373 --> 00:07:36.628
because you're both clueless
as hell to begin with.

00:07:36.628 --> 00:07:37.461
(audience laughs)

00:07:37.461 --> 00:07:39.728
You don't know even what
would make you happy,

00:07:39.728 --> 00:07:42.175
much less what would make
the other person happy.

00:07:42.175 --> 00:07:44.694
And so, you've got to figure
these things out bit by bit

00:07:44.694 --> 00:07:46.745
and then you have to inform each other

00:07:46.745 --> 00:07:49.773
and then you have to be patient enough

00:07:49.773 --> 00:07:52.720
to let your partner do
these things really badly.

00:07:52.720 --> 00:07:53.788
I'll give you another example.

00:07:53.788 --> 00:07:56.871
Sometimes, I see couples sporadically

00:07:57.900 --> 00:07:59.900
in my clinical practice.

00:08:00.919 --> 00:08:03.630
I'm not a couples counselor.

00:08:03.630 --> 00:08:07.847
And so, but sometimes, when
I'm working with someone,

00:08:07.847 --> 00:08:09.715
there's an issue that
needs to be discussed

00:08:09.715 --> 00:08:12.663
with both people, because,
otherwise, it's just stupid.

00:08:12.663 --> 00:08:16.322
And one of the things I
often recommend to people,

00:08:16.322 --> 00:08:20.630
especially once they have
kids, is that they set aside,

00:08:20.630 --> 00:08:24.925
to use an anachronistic
phrase, date nights.

00:08:24.925 --> 00:08:26.580
Well, everyone hates that idea.

00:08:26.580 --> 00:08:29.627
It's like they say, well,
you know, they'll just say,

00:08:29.627 --> 00:08:31.183
I'm not doing that.

00:08:31.183 --> 00:08:32.289
That would be one objection.

00:08:32.289 --> 00:08:33.540
We're just not doing that.

00:08:33.540 --> 00:08:38.404
You know, that's what we
did before we got married.

00:08:38.404 --> 00:08:41.798
They'll say, well, my partner
would never go for that.

00:08:41.798 --> 00:08:44.674
They've got a bunch of excuses
why that isn't going to work.

00:08:44.674 --> 00:08:47.293
And so, I've heard all those excuses.

00:08:47.293 --> 00:08:48.494
I know all of them.

00:08:48.494 --> 00:08:49.742
And then maybe I convince them.

00:08:49.742 --> 00:08:51.936
Yeah, yeah, sure, I know, this is stupid.

00:08:51.936 --> 00:08:54.436
It's awkward, it's artificial.

00:08:55.758 --> 00:08:57.711
That's okay, just try it once.

00:08:57.711 --> 00:08:59.618
So then, they go and try
it, and then they come back

00:08:59.618 --> 00:09:02.027
and they say, we had an
absolutely miserable time.

00:09:02.027 --> 00:09:03.145
Really, we had a miserable time.

00:09:03.145 --> 00:09:06.039
We couldn't agree on what
movie to go to and then,

00:09:06.039 --> 00:09:08.619
she took me to her movie
and I really didn't like it.

00:09:08.619 --> 00:09:09.463
So, we fought all the way home.

00:09:09.463 --> 00:09:11.871
We're never doing that again.

00:09:11.871 --> 00:09:16.159
And I say, wow, really,
you've got 30 years, 400 days.

00:09:16.159 --> 00:09:17.611
That's 12,000 days.

00:09:17.611 --> 00:09:19.559
Okay, so, you're not going to do that.

00:09:19.559 --> 00:09:22.377
You're going to spend the next 12,000 days

00:09:22.377 --> 00:09:26.296
without having any real romantic
evenings and interactions

00:09:26.296 --> 00:09:27.131
with your spouse.

00:09:27.131 --> 00:09:29.162
That's your plan?
(audience laughs)

00:09:29.162 --> 00:09:31.612
I like doing arithmetic with my clients.

00:09:31.612 --> 00:09:32.717
They hate that.

00:09:32.717 --> 00:09:35.553
(audience laughs)
They hate arithmetic.

00:09:35.553 --> 00:09:38.041
It's like, well no, that
sounds like a bad idea.

00:09:38.041 --> 00:09:40.744
I said, okay, well, would you
like some romance in your life

00:09:40.744 --> 00:09:41.883
or are you just done with that?

00:09:41.883 --> 00:09:42.745
(audience laughs)

00:09:42.745 --> 00:09:45.804
Now, really, people can go for a long time

00:09:45.804 --> 00:09:47.317
with no romance at all.

00:09:47.317 --> 00:09:49.888
Say well, no, maybe
we'd like some of that.

00:09:49.888 --> 00:09:50.913
Well, how much?

00:09:50.913 --> 00:09:52.509
Once a year.

00:09:52.509 --> 00:09:53.717
Well, no.

00:09:53.717 --> 00:09:54.875
Once a month?

00:09:54.875 --> 00:09:55.708
Well, no.

00:09:57.257 --> 00:09:58.676
Once every two weeks?

00:09:58.676 --> 00:10:00.292
Well, sometimes people are really busy.

00:10:00.292 --> 00:10:03.299
It's like that beats the hell out of zero.

00:10:03.299 --> 00:10:04.199
Once a week?

00:10:04.199 --> 00:10:05.208
Twice a week?

00:10:05.208 --> 00:10:08.176
Okay, whatever, we're going
to start with a range, okay.

00:10:08.176 --> 00:10:10.175
What would a good evening look like?

00:10:10.175 --> 00:10:14.117
Like, if you could both get
exactly what you wanted,

00:10:14.117 --> 00:10:15.246
what would it look like?

00:10:15.246 --> 00:10:16.369
Well, then they have to think about that,

00:10:16.369 --> 00:10:18.802
because the previous theory was,

00:10:18.802 --> 00:10:21.037
my stupid partner should know what I like,

00:10:21.037 --> 00:10:22.983
and that's what the
partner is thinking too.

00:10:22.983 --> 00:10:24.094
It's like good luck with that,

00:10:24.094 --> 00:10:25.749
because, no, they don't have a clue,

00:10:25.749 --> 00:10:26.907
especially if they're men.

00:10:26.907 --> 00:10:28.099
They don't have a clue.
(audience laughs)

00:10:28.099 --> 00:10:30.486
So you have to tell them what you want

00:10:30.486 --> 00:10:33.066
and how they could
deliver it and vice versa,

00:10:33.066 --> 00:10:34.845
which is very awkward and horrible.

00:10:34.845 --> 00:10:37.962
And then you have to
practice it for six months,

00:10:37.962 --> 00:10:39.425
because, you know, it
takes a lot of practice

00:10:39.425 --> 00:10:42.484
to do something sophisticated really well.

00:10:42.484 --> 00:10:44.139
And then if you do that, it's like, poof!

00:10:44.139 --> 00:10:46.150
You got it for the rest of your life.

00:10:46.150 --> 00:10:48.153
So, it's worth the aggravation.

00:10:48.153 --> 00:10:50.843
You know when the first
time the rat puts its paw

00:10:50.843 --> 00:10:54.594
up on the ladder, it's just
sort of doing it accidentally.

00:10:54.594 --> 00:10:57.072
You can't expect it to do it well.

00:10:57.072 --> 00:10:59.746
And that's exactly how
to view your own progress

00:10:59.746 --> 00:11:01.095
and the progress of your partner.

00:11:01.095 --> 00:11:03.574
It's like let them do
it badly for a while.

00:11:03.574 --> 00:11:05.535
At least, they're doing it.

00:11:05.535 --> 00:11:07.182
So if you think you should do it faster,

00:11:07.182 --> 00:11:09.121
well, look real hard
and see how easy it is

00:11:09.121 --> 00:11:10.795
to change your own behavior,

00:11:10.795 --> 00:11:13.326
because it's really hard to
change your own behavior.

00:11:13.326 --> 00:11:15.779
Everybody says, I'm going to
exercise three times a week!

00:11:15.779 --> 00:11:16.612
(audience laughs)

00:11:16.612 --> 00:11:17.533
At New Year's, and it's like no one does.

00:11:17.533 --> 00:11:20.987
They buy the memberships
and then they feel guilty

00:11:20.987 --> 00:11:23.332
for having it, but they
don't actually go to the gym.

00:11:23.332 --> 00:11:25.436
And that's a perfect example
of how difficult it is

00:11:25.436 --> 00:11:27.549
to transform behavior.

00:11:27.549 --> 00:11:28.382
- Thank you guys so much for watching!

00:11:28.382 --> 00:11:29.973
I'd love to know what did you think,

00:11:29.973 --> 00:11:31.895
what did you take from this
video that you are going

00:11:31.895 --> 00:11:34.495
to apply somehow to your relationships.

00:11:34.495 --> 00:11:36.109
Should we continue this series or not?

00:11:36.109 --> 00:11:39.130
It's still a new one for us
and it's kind of on the bubble.

00:11:39.130 --> 00:11:42.503
I'd like it to, but you guys
ultimately get to decide.

00:11:42.503 --> 00:11:44.435
Let me know, leave it down
in the comments below.

00:11:44.435 --> 00:11:45.898
Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:11:45.898 --> 00:11:46.882
I believe in you.

00:11:46.882 --> 00:11:48.932
I hope you continue to
believe in yourself,

00:11:48.932 --> 00:11:51.107
and whatever your one word is.

00:11:51.107 --> 00:11:52.542
Much love.

00:11:52.542 --> 00:11:53.625
See you soon.

